We have $\sin^2 x + \cos^2 x = 1$ and $\tan x = \frac{\sin x}{\cos x}$.  Let $a = \sin x$ and $b = \cos x$.  Then we have the equations
\begin{align*}
a^2 + b^2 &= 1, \\
\frac{a}{b} &= \frac{b}{a}.
\end{align*}
The second equation implies $a^2 = b^2$, so $a = \pm b$.  Since $a^2 + b^2 = 1$, we have $2a^2 = 1$, so $a^2 = \frac{1}{2}$.  Therefore, $b^2 = \frac{1}{2}$, and
\begin{align*}
\cos^3 x + \cos^2 x &= \frac{1}{2} \left( \cos x + 1 \right) \\
&= \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} + 1 \right) \\
&= \frac{\sqrt{2} + 2}{2\sqrt{2}} \\
